LeanDA security in Lean 4

The library PqDas (PqDas.lean and PqDas/) is Benedikt Wagner's formalization of erasure code commitments, ported from https://github.com/b-wagn/fv-pq-da at commit 4475179c19296acf3469d7d552a3186ad1feda74, and is licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0, a copy of which is in LICENSE.

Changes from that commit: PqDas/Defs.lean is the only file of his library kept, with the definitions LeanDA's proofs use (his arguments of knowledge, Nark, and their extractors; erasure codes, their codewords and the consistency of opened cells), modified when ported to build on VCVio afa6593f15e1e79ecb3cf22fc56191cecb452bb0 and Lean v4.34.1 (measure-based probability notation and lemmas, comments) and since updated with the repository's dependencies; the rest of his library (vector commitments, code checkers, the erasure code commitment and its random-oracle runtime, his reductions, his position-binding, committed-soundness and code-binding proofs and his forking route) was removed and is in his repository. The library LeanDa and the root module LeanDa.lean are new.

LeanDA's end statements (../Statement/LeanDa.lean: completeness, position binding and the membership test's soundness in the random-permutation model, with no argument system) name no definition of PqDas; the framework they come from is his, and their games (positionBindingGame, membershipGame) are derived from his games, under the same license: position binding is his vector commitment's, and the membership test's soundness is his committed soundness, the non-interactive game of his code-binding proof. Their proofs (LeanDa/End.lean) rest on his framework and games: the membership test's soundness is a straight-line argument (LeanDa/StraightTree.lean, LeanDa/StraightBad.lean, LeanDa/Straight.lean, LeanDa/StraightBound.lean) built on his code-binding game, read for an adversary that opens no cell, the argument that accepts everything and the extractor that returns its proof, in place of his forking lemma.
